# OCB-MEM-04 — adversarial full-path query lower bound

## Verdict

**PASS in a declared query model.**

The earlier horizon lower bound is upgraded to a nontrivial lower bound on the **number of observations**.

## Query model

One query returns one independent reset with the complete ordered four-jump destination path.  The tester may perform arbitrary adaptive computation on all returned paths.  Any test based on a weaker bounded path functional is a data-processing of this experiment.

## Explicit rank-3 challenger

A deterministic exploratory EM search on the three-category compression produced a fixed three-component latent-class distribution.  The replay does **not** claim it is the globally closest rank-3 process; it verifies only that this explicit distribution exists.

After lifting back to all 11 destinations while preserving the strict within-category proportions:

- its first-two / last-two block Hankel has numerical rank exactly 3 (and rank<=3 by construction);
- frozen period-2 alias block Hankel has rank 4;
- `KL(P||Q) = 1.0359282401045487e-5`;
- `TV(P,Q) = 0.001037465057253445`.

Thus the composite rank<=3 null contains a distribution extremely close to the rank-4 alias.

## Information-theoretic lower bounds

Bretagnolle--Huber gives

`alpha + beta >= 0.5 exp(-N KL(P||Q))`.

Therefore any procedure with both type-I and type-II error at most 5% needs at least

**155,362 independent four-jump paths**

against this one null member.

The Bhattacharyya/Hellinger tensor-product bound is stronger for equal-prior Bayes error: obtaining Bayes error <=5% requires at least

**321,273 independent four-jump paths**.

These are lower bounds on **all** tests in the declared full-path reset model, not only Hankel statistics.

## Decision

`OCB-MEM-04 = PASS_INSTANCE_QUERY_LOWER_BOUND`.

The result does not give a universal scaling law in hidden dimension `H`; it proves that even at the concrete period-2/rank-4 instance the rank<=3 composite null can be much harder than the earlier narrow challenger families suggested.
